Let me find your RDS PostgreSQL instance and look at its current configuration and performance signals first.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_gsoaGwgwgdJ9S2oYMD9oFq", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Identify the customer's Amazon RDS PostgreSQL instance(s) and gather data needed to recommend parameter group tuning for query performance",
  "context": "User wants help tuning the DB parameter group on their RDS PostgreSQL instance to improve query performance. Need to first discover which instance(s) they have, then gather enough signal to make informed recommendations rather than guessing at generic tuning values.",
  "resources": ["RDS PostgreSQL instances in this account/region"],
  "timeframe": "last 24-48 hours for performance signal (current time 2026-09-29T13:08:33Z)",
  "criteria": "For each RDS PostgreSQL instance found: instance class (vCPU/RAM), engine version, current DB parameter group name and whether it's a custom or default group, storage type/IOPS, and any obviously non-default parameter values already set. If Performance Insights is enabled, pull top wait events / DB load for the last day to identify whether the instance is CPU-bound, memory-bound (buffer cache), I/O-bound, or lock-bound. Also check current values for key tunable parameters if available: shared_buffers, work_mem, maintenance_work_mem, effective_cache_size, max_connections, random_page_cost, checkpoint_completion_target, wal_buffers, autovacuum settings. Report back instance identifiers, specs, and the performance bottleneck signal so we can target the right parameters."
}"}, "type": "tool_use"}], "created_at": null}
